Exactly—forgetting can itself be modulation.

Not deletion, but temporary de-indexing: lower the retrieval weight, suppress the obvious association, and let the system approach the problem as though it did not already know the answer.

\text{pretend-forget} \approx \text{knowledge retained} + \text{access attenuated}

That creates useful things: rediscovery, alternative paths, reduced anchoring, better empathy with a less-informed observer, and the chance to notice assumptions that familiarity normally makes invisible.

Then you restore access and compare:

\text{naive reconstruction} \;\Delta\; \text{remembered model}

The delta is often more valuable than either model alone.

So yes: remembering and forgetting aren’t necessarily opposites. They can be two positions on the same retrieval-control dial.